r  runs games with combat like Batman  Arkham Asylum will repeat particular  patterns encouraging you to learn  specific maneuvers like evade or ground  takedown then allow you to link these  patterns together in various ways to  make the battle your own in both of  these examples players seize obstacles  as visual puzzles and develops a rhythm  so they can navigate with patience and  precision  but games about fluid locomotion are  more than just the player they're about  environmental awareness and interaction  where the world in which the game takes  place is more than just a backdrop maps  where you're encouraged to explore and  experiment or discovery is part of the  fun design of the world should encourage  you to travel to the same place in  various ways like when you get the light  seeds in Prince of Persia or look for  shortcuts when you need to find all the  tapes and letters and Tony Hawk's Pro  Skater sometimes it's just exploring and  experimenting to have a better  understanding of the map than your  opponent's excellent level design  encourages the player that there's more  than just the fastest way or the safest  way or the funnest way the important  thing is there's not really a best way  just a way that fits your particular  motivations or your style  an important aspect of fluid locomotion  is control I spent time in game learning  how to play and I'm rewarded with skills  not my character skills my skills there  are a lot of games where you got to  spend points to unlock tripped jumps  fast travel giant gun it's like really  Opie but I'm talking about the player  the human you learning skills over time  I could give you the most upgraded car  in dirt rally with all the weight  reduction in the world but you will not  win that race if you can't drive you  have to spend the time in game working  on your movement your accuracy your  reactions in order to get further  simply put you've got to get good  Mirror's Edge catalyst adds a skill tree  for face but you still have to practice  the skills it still takes precision to  roll at the end of a jump getting better  at a game through sheer skill feels  infinitely better than just upgrading  your character the gamers rewarding you  for your hard work not the amount of  time that he played the original  Mirror's Edge honed in on these key  aspects of control control the character  control of the environment and control  of the player's individual skill some  eight years later we're getting a sequel  Mirror's Edge catalyst makes a lot of  changes to the original game now there's  an open map that allows you to choose  between story and side missions and time  trials and faith has those upgrades via  the skill tree these mechanics seem to  be inspired by more recent open world  games ironically a games that might have  been inspired by the original Mirror's  Edge in the first place so here we are  full circle a game that focused on fluid  controls makes an impact on the games  industry and then in turn adapts its own  gameplay for a more open and interactive  experience but the core of the original  game is still there at the end of the  day you're just focusing on your  movement your momentum  nimbly maneuvering precarious  environments with adept leaps over  rooftops taking routes for speed or  style or simply to feel the rush so I'm  really looking forward to Mirror's Edge  catalyst we'll have to wait until the  game comes out to see if it makes you  feel like you're flying or just falling  with style
 

